All right. Here’s a take. Long ago, the corn snake had no legend. It slipped through the fields quiet and unnoticed. One day, cornered by a fox, it puffed itself up and shook its tail in the dry leaves. The leaves rattled like a warning, and the fox hesitated. The snake survived so it practiced the rattle. Night after night, season after season, telling its young the story. We are the ones who rattle. We are dangerous. Some believed so hard they mistook the story for blood. But Every so often, a hatchling would ask, if we are truly venom, why must we rehearse the sound? And an elder would answer, because the sound saved us, but it’s still a sound. The lesson wasn’t that the snake was false, but that survival habits aren’t identities. They can be set down, revised, or repurposed without losing oneself. And the field, the fox, the leaves, reality itself, kept grading the story, moment by moment.